// R
// The TypeScript discriminated-union idiom, and D1023's ruling in one file: two struct arms
// that share a field-NAME set and differ only in a literal-typed field are a LEGAL union, and
// `is` over them is a DISCRIMINANT-VALUE test.
//
// THIS FILE WAS AN `@emit-error` FIXTURE AND THE RULING REVERSED IT. Its old header argued
// the pair had to stay loud: "a tag VALUE is not a runtime discriminator, because a bare
// literal field stores as a plain string slot. Making this pair work needs the tag to carry an
// interned identity — the un-named-literal ATOM rep, A5c — which is a representation decision,
// not a widening of this exemption." The representation decision was made
// (DECISIONS §"`is A` over same-shape struct arms is a DISCRIMINANT-VALUE test") and it did
// NOT need the atom rep: the string slot already holds the member's own characters, so the
// discriminator was in the value all along and only the compare was missing.
//
// `c is C` now lowers to the arm's tag test GUARDING a `struct.get` of `kind` compared against
// `"circle"` — an `if (result i32)` and not an `i32.and`, because both operands of an `i32.and`
// are evaluated and the `ref.cast` inside the read would then run on arms of every other shape.
//
// The value is built through `R`, so the answer is `R`. It is `R` at both spellings: `is` and
// the `c.kind == "circle"` discriminant comparison compute the same arm set by construction.
type C = { kind: "circle", size: f64 }
type R = { kind: "rect", size: f64 }
type S = C | R

const c: S = { kind: "rect", size: 2.0 }
if c is C { print("C") } else { print("R") }
